course: dictionary
lesson: 9031
title: Slangy words and where they belong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Slang is very informal, everyday language, like «gonna», «cool» or «mate». Slangy words are the words that sound like slang. Friends use them all the time, and writers use them in stories, but they are not right everywhere.
explain: Writers choose words for their reader and the moment. A slangy word can show a character's age, where they live or how they feel. «Soz» is a quick, slangy way to say sorry, and it often sounds like you don't really mean it.
explain: Essays, letters to the head teacher and exam answers need proper, formal words. A line like «I'm gonna do my best» is fine in a chat with your mate, but in an essay it sounds too casual. Choosing words that fit the situation is called choosing the right register.
fact: Slangy means using slang: very informal, everyday words and phrases.
fact: «Soz» is a slangy way to say sorry.
fact: «Popped his clogs», «snuffed it», «brown bread» and «cashed in his chips» are all slangy ways of saying someone died.
fact: Slangy talk is fine with friends, but it is too slangy for an essay or a letter to the head teacher.
fact: In a story, slangy talk can show a character's age, place or mood.
exercise: Which word is slangy? | tremendous | *cool | magnificent | splendid
exercise: «Soz» is a slangy way to say what? | goodbye | hello | *sorry | thank you
exercise: Where is the best place to use slangy words? | A letter to the head teacher | An exam answer | An essay for your teacher | *A chat with your best mate
test: «The old man popped his clogs last winter.» What does popped his clogs mean? | Got a new pair of shoes | *Died | Moved house | Fell asleep in the snow
test: Which is right for an exam answer? | Brown bread | Soz and mate | Gonna and cool | *Formal, proper words
exam: Why might a writer use slangy talk in a story? | To copy the words of an exam question | To hide the meaning from the reader | *To show a character's age, place or mood | Only to make the story harder to read
